Handover from karr #117, which fixed only lib/File/SOPS.pm (Encrypted.pm and
Format/YAML.pm were held by karr #116 at the time and were off limits).

Every recursive tree walk in this distribution warns "Deep recursion on
subroutine" once it passes perls fixed threshold of 100, on documents sops
accepts. #117 gave the walks in SOPS.pm "no warnings recursion" plus a depth
bound of its own ($File::SOPS::MAX_DEPTH, go-yamls 10000). Measured on a
265-level alias chain (a document sops accepts): 505 warning lines and 63 KB
of STDERR before, 168 lines and 24 KB after -- and every remaining line comes
from File::SOPS::Encrypted::_canonical_floats, which runs inside every emit.

What is left to do, in the files #117 could not touch:

  lib/File/SOPS/Encrypted.pm   _canonical_floats
  lib/File/SOPS/Format/YAML.pm _reject_comment_leaves, _restring_non_finite_leaves
  lib/File/SOPS/Format/JSON.pm the same pair, if it carries them

Two changes each, both mechanical and both already made in SOPS.pm:

  1. "no warnings recursion" as the first statement of the walk. Per sub, not
     per file, so nothing outside the walk loses the warning.
  2. Carry the key path as ONE array pushed and popped, instead of building
     [ @$path, $k ] per level. The copy is quadratic in depth: measured in
     SOPS.pms _sorted_leaves, 4000 levels took 101s and 410 MB, and the same
     walk with a shared path reaches 10000 levels in 17ms and 24 MB. Copy the
     path only where a leaf keeps it.

t/45-deep-document-is-quiet.t section 2 is a TODO block that pins exactly this:
it asserts that a full File::SOPS->encrypt of a 265-level document writes no
deep-recursion warning at all, and it is expected to fail until this lands.
Turn the TODO off in the same commit.

_canonical_floats does not need a depth bound of its own: SOPS.pms guards run
ahead of every emit. It only needs the two lines above.
